The user says "The game does not need to be playable yet. It just needs to load in a browser without console errors."

So we need to produce index.html with basic structure: DOCTYPE, html lang, head with meta charset utf-8, title set to game title (maybe from design). The ORBIT SHIFT concept: "The dot auto‑advances while rotating around a central axis. Each tap toggles between an inner and outer orbital radius, letting you dodge obstacles that appear at specific distances."

Thus title: "Orbit Shift". Description should be something like that. Controls list: probably "Tap to toggle orbit radius", "Press R or click Restart". Possibly "Press R or click Restart" for restart.

The tags: presumably "arcade", "orbit", "dodging"? The instructions for tags optional but we can include e.g., ["arcade","orbit","dodging"]. But the tags must be lowercase and optional. Good.

Meta.json must match schema: title string 3-80 chars, description 20-400 chars, controls non-empty array of strings, tags optional.

Now index.html must have header with h1 same title, p with description, ul of controls each li matching array order.

Also must have canvas with id "game". Must have restart button.

Script stub must set up basic functions but not fatal errors. It needs to implement the sections: CONFIG, STATE, RESET, LOOP/UPDATE, RENDER, INPUT, AUDIO, OBSERVABLE CONTRACT, BOOT. The reference example includes these and more.

Since we need stub script with minimal content but must not error out. Must define functions called in boot: reset(); So stub reset must exist. Should also define startLoop and loop/tick functions? Possibly simpler, we can define empty stub functions that do nothing but not reference undefined variables.

For minimal viable, we can just create:

- Global variables: let loopId = null;

- function reset() { if (loopId !== null) cancelAnimationFrame(loopId); startLoop(); }

- function startLoop() { if (loopId !== null) cancelAnimationFrame(loopId); function tick(now) { try { /* nothing */ } catch(e) {} loopId = requestAnimationFrame(tick); } loopId = requestAnimationFrame(tick); }

- In publishState we set window.__game with proper shape: state "playing"? The spec says state should be "menu" initially perhaps? But in PLAYABLE contract, "state" should be "menu", "playing", "gameover". Several games start at "playing" perhaps. Let's set initial state to "menu". But we must set objective too.

The stub script can set a simple placeholder: player at center, visible true. Score 0. state "menu". Then we need to call publishState() each frame after. For initial boot, must set window.__game at start. So after definitions, call reset(); That will call startLoop which will loop and call publishState each tick. So contract is satisfied.

Make sure script doesn't refer to any undefined var.

We need to follow the same comment divider sections: // === CONFIG ============================================================= etc. But not required for validator; it's for style.

The reference example includes heavy code; we can keep same structure with minimal variables. We may include `const W = 800, H = 600;` for canvas dimensions. Provide canvas = document.getElementById('game'); ctx = canvas.getContext('2d'); That will reference canvas id, which exists.

Inside render, perhaps clear canvas and draw something non-blank to satisfy requirement that canvas has non-blank pixels after 3 seconds. The validator 6 says "Canvas has non-blank pixels after 3 seconds". If we render nothing (clear rect), canvas will be blank (background color maybe set). In astro the background draws a fillRect with some color, that qualifies as non-blank. So in render we must fill a background color. That is enough.

Therefore we need render() that fills background.

We may also fill a small dot representing the player.

